# The root `.gitignore` ends every new JSON in the tree (`*.json`), which is right
# for runtime data and wrong for one file in this directory.
#
# `dup_calibration.json` is the labelled calibration set the retrieval eval's
# near-duplicate threshold is fitted on (#2271), and it is the artifact an
# acceptance clause says is committed: the threshold in `eval/dup_detect.py` is a
# number, and a number with no labelled set behind it is a vibe — a reader who
# cannot see the 40 pairs and the two disclosed misses cannot check whether 0.6
# was measured or guessed, and neither can the next round. `tests/test_eval_dup_
# slot_metric.py::test_committed_calibration_is_at_least_thirty_pairs_and_is_
# reproduced` re-derives the agreement from the rows on every run, so the
# committed copy cannot drift from the predicate without the suite going red; it
# is committed rather than regenerated at test time because regenerating it needs
# the live vault, which the gate's test run must not read.
#
# Scoped to filenames and never to `eval/*.json`: a runtime dump dropped
# here by accident stays ignored, which is what the root rule is for. The
# root file itself is off-limits to the self-modification loop (denied in
# scripts/automod/spec.py), so a file like this one is the only route that does
# not end in a human typing `git add -f` for every regenerated calibration.
# `tests/fixtures/.gitignore` is the same device for the same reason (#2269).
!dup_calibration.json

# The two committed fixtures the skill-catalog overlap instrument reads (#2440):
# `skill_entity_overlap_pairs.json` is the scored pair set whose production figures
# are copied into `tests/test_skill_entity_overlap.py`, and
# `skill_entity_overlap_pair_bodies.json` holds the SKILL.md bodies behind them. Both
# are tracked today — #2440 added them past the root rule — and both were still
# reported ignored (`git check-ignore -q --no-index` exit 0, last match
# `.gitignore:42 *.json`) with no negation beside this one.
#
# What that leaves broken is not a regeneration: `git add -A` does stage a change to a
# tracked file the rule matches. It is any path not tracked yet — a renamed fixture
# family, a regenerated sibling, a fresh clone, anything after `git rm --cached` —
# which `git add -A` skips without a word. That is #1701's chrome manifest, and the
# pair of assertions in
# `tests/test_skill_entity_overlap.py::test_an_overlap_fixture_is_not_ignored` is what
# keeps these two from quietly becoming it again.
!skill_entity_overlap_pairs.json
!skill_entity_overlap_pair_bodies.json
